1
00:00:01,400 --> 00:00:07,069
In 1972, Cybersyn's engineers went from factory to factory across Chile.

2
00:00:08,769 --> 00:00:13,606
In each, with its workers and managers, they chose about five numbers to watch.

3
00:00:14,906 --> 00:00:18,239
If one went out of its range, the software would send a warning.

4
00:00:19,739 --> 00:00:23,637
Stafford Beer and the team had to decide who that warning would go to.

5
00:00:25,337 --> 00:00:32,800
If every warning went to Santiago, a few people there would be buried in small problems, and factories would wait for orders.

6
00:00:34,300 --> 00:00:41,218
President Allende had insisted the system be decentralising, with workers taking part, and not bureaucratic.

7
00:00:42,718 --> 00:00:49,191
Factories needed room to solve their own problems, and the centre needed to hear only of the ones they couldn't.

8
00:00:50,891 --> 00:00:56,790
So, in Beer's design, a warning would go first to the factory's own manager, and nobody else.

9
00:00:58,090 --> 00:01:00,993
They would have a set time to deal with it, in their own way.

10
00:01:02,693 --> 00:01:08,843
Only if it wasn't solved in time would the team tell the next level: that industry's committee, at CORFO.

11
00:01:10,343 --> 00:01:14,876
The engineers chose each factory's time. Beer called it designing freedom.

12
00:01:16,576 --> 00:01:24,635
Each factory, Beer argued, would keep almost complete control of its work, and the centre would step in only on serious problems.

13
00:01:26,335 --> 00:01:31,558
At the utility, Inés opens the catalogue: the list of every table on the platform.

14
00:01:32,858 --> 00:01:39,050
Of its 412 tables, 300 name the data team as their owner, because it built them.

15
00:01:46,550 --> 00:01:50,653
One of them is hosting capacity: how much solar each street can take.

16
00:01:52,153 --> 00:01:56,661
On the map, it's Grace's: her engineers defined it, and how to work it out.

17
00:01:58,261 --> 00:02:03,050
The data team built the table from their method, and households check it on the website.

18
00:02:04,750 --> 00:02:11,354
In May, the engineers found older cables carry less, and changed their method. The table kept the old one.

19
00:02:13,454 --> 00:02:18,611
The engineers didn't see the table as theirs to tell, and the data team had no way to know.

20
00:02:20,511 --> 00:02:25,611
For four months, the website told households their street could take more solar than it could.

21
00:02:27,511 --> 00:02:32,764
Ninety families bought panels, then found they could send back only part of the power they made.

22
00:02:35,064 --> 00:02:40,506
Nobody was careless. The table's owner was the one team that couldn't know what its numbers meant.

23
00:02:44,206 --> 00:02:51,018
Who owns a table is for the owners to decide, so Inés brings them one rule, to make the platform match the map.

24
00:02:52,318 --> 00:02:59,427
Every table belongs to a data product, and each product to whoever owns the capability that creates its data.

25
00:03:01,327 --> 00:03:06,811
Planning the network creates hosting capacity, so its product is Grace's, method and all.

26
00:03:08,111 --> 00:03:12,205
Serving customers creates accounts, so theirs is the head of retail's.

27
00:03:13,505 --> 00:03:20,148
A measure that crosses domains belongs to the enterprise domain: for the regulator's measures, that's Ama.

28
00:03:21,648 --> 00:03:30,162
The data team builds and runs every product. The centre keeps only what all domains share: the platform, the catalogue, a few rules.

29
00:03:31,862 --> 00:03:36,274
As in Beer's design, each domain solves its own data's problems first.

30
00:03:37,774 --> 00:03:41,132
The owners agree, and the rule goes on the map with its date.

31
00:03:43,832 --> 00:03:48,514
A data product is data made for others to use, with someone who answers for it.

32
00:03:50,214 --> 00:03:54,145
At the utility, a product is published only when it has five things.

33
00:03:55,445 --> 00:03:58,000
An owner: for hosting capacity, Grace.

34
00:03:59,100 --> 00:04:03,738
Documentation: what it means, in the business's words, and how it's worked out.

35
00:04:05,038 --> 00:04:09,336
Lineage: where each number comes from, back to the network map's cables.

36
00:04:10,636 --> 00:04:16,676
A contract: fresh every Monday, and a new method only as a new version, with readers told first.

37
00:04:18,176 --> 00:04:22,882
And an approved way in: readers ask in the catalogue, and the owner decides.

38
00:04:24,182 --> 00:04:28,204
The one catalogue lists them all: what exists, and who answers for it.

39
00:04:31,704 --> 00:04:36,808
Ask how to keep data, and you'll hear four words: warehouse, lakehouse, mesh and fabric.

40
00:04:38,108 --> 00:04:41,186
They sound like rivals. They answer different questions.

41
00:04:42,886 --> 00:04:48,897
A warehouse is one store, modelled for reports, usually built and run by one central team.

42
00:04:50,397 --> 00:04:58,704
A lakehouse keeps files in an open format, with a warehouse's tables and controls on top, for reports and machine learning alike.

43
00:05:00,204 --> 00:05:09,077
A mesh isn't a technology: each domain owns its data as products, on a self-serve platform, under rules the domains agree together.

44
00:05:10,777 --> 00:05:17,288
A fabric uses metadata to find, connect and govern data across many stores, automating what it can.

45
00:05:18,788 --> 00:05:24,521
So they mix. The utility's target is a mesh of owners, on its lakehouse, with one catalogue.

46
00:05:26,221 --> 00:05:29,410
One platform or several is the next series' question.

47
00:05:32,110 --> 00:05:36,872
Inés draws the data as it is, the baseline, and as it should be, the target.

48
00:05:38,372 --> 00:05:44,538
Today, the data team owns 300 tables, few have a contract, and access means an email.

49
00:05:46,038 --> 00:05:53,528
In the target, every table is in a product with a business owner, or retired, and the data team owns only the platform.

50
00:05:55,228 --> 00:05:59,477
Each difference is a gap, and goes into the roadmap with an owner and a quarter.

51
00:06:00,977 --> 00:06:06,178
It starts where it hurts: hosting capacity first, then the products with the most readers.

52
00:06:07,478 --> 00:06:10,097
Each domain has three months to claim its tables.

53
00:06:11,397 --> 00:06:17,170
What nobody claims by then is archived, its readers told first, and retired after that.

54
00:06:19,070 --> 00:06:24,193
The domains solve it first. The centre steps in only where it isn't solved in time.

55
00:06:27,493 --> 00:06:34,222
On the data practice's map, the domains already hold their concepts: the network, retail and what they share.

56
00:06:35,722 --> 00:06:39,745
Now the enterprise domain joins them, for the measures that cross them.

57
00:06:41,245 --> 00:06:47,982
Each data product goes in its domain, drawn as a product: something the business offers, under a contract.

58
00:06:49,682 --> 00:06:55,258
Hosting capacity brings together the data it offers and its contract, and Grace owns it.

59
00:06:56,958 --> 00:07:03,038
Every table in the catalogue now points to its product, so the map and the platform say the same thing.

60
00:07:08,938 --> 00:07:16,476
In Beer's design, each factory would solve its own problems first, and the centre would hear only of those not solved in time.

61
00:07:18,176 --> 00:07:22,511
At the utility, each domain now solves its own data's problems first.

62
00:07:23,811 --> 00:07:30,943
In three months, the domains claimed 246 of the 300 tables, in 31 data products.

63
00:07:32,443 --> 00:07:37,790
Nobody claimed the other 54. They were archived, and nobody has asked for one back.

64
00:07:39,290 --> 00:07:45,349
When Grace's engineers next changed their method, hosting capacity changed with it, as a new version.

65
00:07:46,649 --> 00:07:51,140
Its readers knew a week ahead, and no family bought panels its street couldn't take.

66
00:07:52,840 --> 00:07:58,836
Each domain now owns its data. But with so many owners, who keeps them all speaking one language?
